  "draftIsFinal": false,
  "summary": "I wrote the solar-system explorer in `src/main.ts`. It passes `typecheck`, but I couldn't run it, so I haven't seen it render, and the camera and lighting are unconfirmed in a browser.\n\n- **Scene:** a glowing star with a corona and bloom, on a starfield of about 800 instanced emissive spheres. There are 8 planets (Mercury to Neptune), each at a different distance, size, colour and speed, with inner planets faster. Speed follows 0.5·√(88/period days) rad/s, so Mercury takes about 12.6 s per orbit.\n- **Planet looks:** each planet has procedural vertex colours (rocky noise, gas-giant bands, a continents-and-clouds Earth). Planets spin on their own axis. Earth has one moon, Jupiter has two, and Saturn has a tilted banded ring. Each planet has a thin orbit-line ribbon.\n- **Camera:** camera control is a custom rig on `app.camera.use(...)`. If `app.camera` is undefined the camera would stay fixed. Focus eases over 1.5 s (cubic in/out) to a close view and keeps following the planet. Escape or a click on empty space eases back to the overview.\n- **Input:** I pick planets with my own screen-space projection, with a minimum 9 px hit radius so the tiny overview planets are clickable. In the overview, drag orbits and scroll zooms, limited to distances 70–480. A button toggles 'Pause' and 'Play' and freezes orbits, spin and moons.\n- **Info panel:** the DOM panel shows the planet's name, orbital period in Earth days and distance in million km.\n- **Exposed API:** `window.__TASK_STATE__` is a read-only object with live getters. `__focusPlanet(name)` and `__unfocus()` call the same code as a click on a planet or on empty space. `__EVAL_READY__` is set after the first frames are drawn.\n\nThe pose I set takes effect one frame late, so the focus view can sit slightly off-centre on a fast inner planet.\n\n",
